# G028 — Vigenere / Vernam / Caesar Ciphers

*v2. See `ciphers.md` for prose: reversible transformation with a
secret, the no-key to perfect-secrecy spectrum mirrors trust in
the channel, small key spaces fall to brute force, Vernam shows the
cost of perfect security.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Private Agent-to-Agent Channel

[private-channel @sender @receiver @key
    IN ORDER TO
        carry a private message through a public choreography
        by encrypting at the sender and decrypting at the receiver
        with a previously-negotiated shared key

    @plaintext <- @sender{compose-sensitive-report}

    @cipher <- [cipher[encrypt
                         {alg: "vigenere",
                          text: @plaintext,
                          key: @key}]]

    ;; @cipher can pass through any broadcast channel
    [broadcast[send {to: @receiver, payload: @cipher}]]

    @received <- [@receiver[inbox-drain]] last
    @plain    <- [cipher[decrypt
                           {alg: "vigenere",
                            text: @received,
                            key: @key}]]

    @plain where equal to @plaintext  -> #channel-verified
]

---

## Verb Surface

[cipher
    encrypt            {alg, text, key}           ;; "caesar" | "vigenere" | "vernam"
    decrypt            {alg, text, key}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: key-space spectrum `where` trust decides algorithm

[cipher[encrypt {alg: @a, text: @t, key: @k}]
    @a
        where "caesar"
            -> apply fixed shift (tiny key space — Hello-World encryption)
        where "vigenere"
            -> repeat @k cyclically and shift per character (good-enough)
        where "vernam"
            -> require @k at least as long as @t, never reused
               (perfect secrecy; expensive at scale)
    IN ORDER TO
        match algorithm to trust level —
        Caesar catches accidents; Vigenere resists casual attack;
        Vernam is provably secure but impractical for most channels
]

Same discipline G044 Remote Login applies to authentication —
multiple strengths exist and the choreography picks per context.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: reversible `where` decrypt ∘ encrypt = id

[cipher[decrypt {alg: @a, text: [cipher[encrypt {alg: @a, text: @t, key: @k}]],
                  key: @k}]
    returns @t exactly
    IN ORDER TO
        guarantee the round-trip contract that makes a cipher useful
        as a communication primitive between agents
]

G017 Pig Latin was the reversible transformation without a key.
G028 adds the secret. The round-trip contract is identical; what
differs is who can reverse it.

---

## Demo

[demo
    -> [cipher[encrypt {alg: "caesar", text: "HELLO", key: 3}]]
    ;; -> "KHOOR"

    -> [cipher[decrypt {alg: "caesar", text: "KHOOR", key: 3}]]
    ;; -> "HELLO"

    -> [cipher[encrypt {alg: "vigenere",
                          text: "ATTACKATDAWN", key: "LEMON"}]]
    ;; -> "LXFOPVEFRNHR"
]
